/me spots a bug:

I suspect the line:
	for (n = 0; n < rn2(6)+3; n++) {
is wrong.  Unless rn2 is *very* strange, a new random number will be 
generated every time you go round the loop, but you probably wanted:
	xchar count = rn2(6)+3;
	for (n = 0; n < count; n++) {
or something along those lines.

